BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260725T180000Z
DTEND:20260725T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260725T000000Z
SUMMARY:Durham Grown: The Durham County Library Garden Club
DESCRIPTION:Exploring Summer Light in the Garden! \n\nJoin Jeanine Brandi 
 of J. Bee Studios to learn how to make the most of these long\, light 
 filled summer days! Learn how to take beautiful photographs that capture 
 your garden and all of its abundance! You will learn to use whatever 
 equipment you have to document your garden or other places that inspire 
 you. Jeanine will present photographs from different gardens she has 
 documented through the years as examples. You will learn practical ways to 
 improve your photo skills to savor the seasons and capture these moments 
 before they pass! \n\nJeanine Brandi is the founder of J.Bee Studios- 
 specializing in landscape design\, photography\, and flowers. She has a 
 Bachelor of Science in Photography from Syracuse University and worked in 
 the photography field for many years before returning to school for 
 Landscape Architecture. She received a master's degree in Landscape 
 Architecture from Rhode Island School of Design. She enjoys creating and 
 documenting landscapes to live in\, focused on interaction\, sustainability 
 and stewardship. \n \n\nConnect with Jeanine on Instagram @j.bee.studios or 
 on the web at J. Bee Studios or JBrandi\n\n\n\nSaturday July 25th starting 
 at 2 pm in the auditorium at the Durham County Main Library located at 300 
 North Roxboro Street.  \n\n\n\nParking is available in the library lot or 
 at the North Queen Street Garage which is locate at 115 North Queen Street 
 which is located directly across the street from the library. Parking is 
 free in the garage. Bring your parking ticket to a library service point 
 for validation.     \n\n\n\nImage description: A soft pink almond blossom 
 taken at Fat Uncle Farms in Winter 2012 \n\nPhoto credit Jeanine Brandi\, 
 J. Bee Studios
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Megan Koslofsky":MAILTO:mkoslofsky@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Arts & Crafts, Classes & Lectures, Durham, Environmental, Gardening, Maker, STEAM
UID:LibCal-14288-15882796
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/15882796
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260725T180000Z
DTEND:20260725T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260725T000000Z
SUMMARY:Game Mastering 101
DESCRIPTION:Due to a scheduling conflict\, this program series has been 
 moved to July 25 - August 22. \n\n \n\nAre you interested in becoming a 
 game or dungeon master but don't know how? Maybe you're scared you'll mess 
 up or maybe you don't have a group of players to GM for. Either way\, this 
 is the place for you! Become a master game master in this program series 
 with experienced GM and game studies professor DA Hall!\n\nThroughout the 
 series\, participants will learn the basics of GMing\, get comfortable with 
 collaborative storytelling\, practice running their own one-shots\, learn 
 how to homebrew their own campaigns\, and more.\n\n \n\nJuly 25\, 2 - 2:30 
 p.m.\n\nGM Fundamentals Interactive Lecture\n\nThis first session is an 
 interactive lecture that will cover GM fundamentals such as basic 
 terminology\, collaborative storytelling\, story hooks and worldbuilding\, 
 challenges and progression\, game balance and random chance\, and managing 
 players before\, during\, and after the play session. \n\n \n\nAugust 1\, 2 
 - 5 p.m.\n\nGuided Wanderhome Play\n\nIn this session\, participants will 
 be split into groups of 3-4 and will play Wanderhome\, a system without a 
 primary GM\, to get a feel for collaborative storytelling and 
 worldbuilding\, as well as playing different characters. Each group will 
 have a veteran player to gently guide them.\n\n \n\nAugust 8\, 2 - 3:30 
 p.m.\n\nWorkshop on Running a D&D Campaign\n\nThis workshop will cover how 
 to structure single play sessions\, structuring full campaigns\, planning 
 vs prepping\, timing\, improvisation\, and more.\n\n \n\nAugust 15\, 10 
 a.m. - 1 p.m. OR 2 p.m. - 5 p.m.\n\nOne Shot Playtest\n\nIn this session\, 
 participants will get the chance to playtest the first session of their 
 campaigns and receive gentle\, constructive feedback from experienced 
 players. Experienced GMs will be there to observe and answer questions. 
 \n\n \n\nAugust 22\, 2 - 3:30 p.m.\n\nHow to Homebrew and Q&A\n\nThis 
 lecture will cover game design fundamentals\, iterative design practices\, 
 and when and why to homebrew-- from on-the-fly tweaks to custom 
 items/subclasses to total overhaul mods. Then\, there will be a Q&A for 
 participants to ask any remaining questions.\n\n \n\nDA Hall holds a 
 doctorate in English & Comparative Literature from the University of North 
 Carolina at Chapel Hill where they are currently employed as a Teaching 
 Assistant Professor. Dr. Hall's dissertation examines the videogame as a 
 key site for the negotiation of Japanese cultural identity within the 
 globalized neoliberal order that coalesced in the wake of the Cold War\, 
 presented through a genealogy of Japanese videogames. DA Hall has also 
 written and presented extensively on FromSoftware’s Soulsborne series\, 
 and is co-editor of Beyond the Border of Japanese Video Games\, a volume 
 currently under review that explores Japanese video game perspectives that 
 reveal\, remediate\, and resist the imperialist logics of Western 
 worlding.\n
LOCATION:South Meeting Room\, South Regional Library (4505 S. Alston Ave)
ORGANIZER;CN="Elle F.":MAILTO:kfarmer@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Classes & Lectures, Gaming, Social
UID:LibCal-14288-16788034
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16788034
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260725T180000Z
DTEND:20260725T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260725T000000Z
SUMMARY:The Blueprint Production Presents Sister Rosetta Tharpe: Thee Blueprint and so much more!
DESCRIPTION:This dynamic 60 minute immersive multimedia presentation 
 illuminates the extraordinary life and legacy of Sister Rosetta 
 Tharpe—the true blueprint of modern music. Long before rock 'n' roll had 
 a name\, Sister Rosetta wielded her electric guitar with unmatched power\, 
 blending sacred gospel with audacious showmanship that would define 
 generations of music genres to come. \n\nThrough active learning games\, 
 fun discussions\, projected visual storytelling\, and digital archival 
 footage of her electrifying performances\, audiences will discover how this 
 pioneering Black woman broke every barrier within the turbulent 
 1930s-1940s\, creating a blueprint that stretches from gospel to punk rock. 
 By program's end\, participants will move beyond the limiting "Godmother of 
 Rock 'n' Roll" title to recognize her as the foundational "BLUEPRINT" whose 
 faith\, talent\, and fearless use of spectacle shaped the soundtrack of 
 American Popular culture and its music! This interdisciplinary arts 
 experience challenges what we think we know about music history while 
 celebrating innovation\, faith\, and self-determination.
LOCATION:SLW Meeting Room - Full Room\, Stanford L. Warren Branch Library (1201 Fayetteville St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Carter Cue":MAILTO:ccue@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-17024848
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17024848
END:VEVENT
